They must not honor their parents with their gift. To make God's word of no effect to follow your own traditions.
O Impostors! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said,
These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. As for them, they know only human rules." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him. He said to them, "Listen and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make you unclean. What comes out of your mouth is what makes you unclean.
And so, the disciples came to him. They asked, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angry when they heard this?"
Jesus replied, "The plants my Father in heaven did not plant will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads another blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this thing to us."
Don't you understand yet? Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that goes into the mouth goes to the stomach. Then it leaves the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth, those are from the heart. Those are the things that make you 'unclean'.
Wicked thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and other sexual sins. So also theft, false testimony, and lying to others.
These are the things that make you 'unclean'. However, eating without washing your hands does not make you 'unclean'. "The Faith of the Woman from Canaan"
Jesus went out from Galilee and departed into the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A woman from Canaan was living near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon has control over my daughter. She is suffering greatly."
Jesus didn't say a word. So his disciples came to him. They asked him, "Send her away. She's always crying out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
And after the woman fell to her knees in front of him. "Oh God! Help me!" she said.
He said, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
"Ay, God," she said. "Even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you as you desire." And her daughter was instantly healed. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus went out from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up on a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds brought him. They brought blind people and those who could not walk. They also brought the disabled, those who could not speak, and many others. They laid them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak were speaking. The disabled were healed. Those who could not walk were walking. Those who were blind could see. Thus the people praised the Lord of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said to them, "I feel a deep concern for these people. They have been with me for three days. They have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do that, they will collapse on their way home."
His disciples answered him, saying, "There is nothing here but five loaves and two fish." And he said, "Bring them here
"How many loaves have you?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they said, "and a few small fish."
Jesus told the group to sit on the ground.
Take the seven loaves of bread and the fish, and give thanks. Then He broke them and distributed them to the disciples, and the disciples distributed them to the people.
They all ate and rested. Later, the students collected seven baskets of leftovers.
The number of men who ate was 40,000. Women and children also ate more.
After Jesus cried out to the multitude, he departed, entered into a vessel, and went into the region of Magadan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
Answer, "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, 'The weather is going to be fine. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning she says, 'Today will be stormy. The sky is red and cloudy.' She knows the meaning of what she sees in the sky. But she cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
"A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign; yet no sign shall be given to it but the sign of Jonah." And Jesus left them and departed. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. They forgot to take the bread.
Beware, Jesus said to them. "Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked about this among themselves. They said, "He must be saying it because we didn't bring bread."
Jesus knew what to say. He said, "Your faith is too small! Why do you talk to each other about not having bread?
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets of fragments you collected?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets of fragments you gathered?
How can you not understand? I was not talking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
And after the disciples understood that Jesus was not referring to being careful of the yeast in the bread. He was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees taught. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went about the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
They said, "Some say John the Baptist; others say Elijah; and others Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"And you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the living God."
Jesus replied, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For it was not with ordinary human means that you came to realize this. My Father in heaven revealed it to you."
Here is what I tell you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
To give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus called his disciples and told them not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus spoke about his coming death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what was going to happen to him. He told them, "The Son of Man must undergo great suffering, and be rejected by the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed and rise to life on the third day."
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "God forbid, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. For you are not setting your mind on the things of God, but on the things of men."
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "If anyone loves me, he must follow me, and he must deny himself and take up his cross and follow me."
If he loves to save himself, he will lose it. But if he loses himself for my sake, he will find it.
What's the point if someone wins the whole world and loses their soul? Or what could they possibly exchange for their soul?
The Son of Man is coming in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will repay each person according to what he has done.
What I am about to tell you is true. Some of those standing here will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus' appearance changes.
After six days, Jesus took Peter and John, James's brother, up on a high mountain. There they were all alone.
In front of them, his form changed. His face became like the sun. His clothing became white like light.
Suddenly, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three tents - one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And as Peter continued to speak, a bright cloud enveloped them. A voice from the cloud said, "This is my son, whom I love. I am very pleased with him. Listen to him!"
When the students heard this, they were very afraid. They fell face down to the ground.
Jesus came and touched them. He said to them, "Arise," "Do not be afraid."
When they raised their heads, they did not see anyone who had passed Jesus.
They went down the mountain. On the way down, Jesus told them what to do. "Don't tell anyone until you have seen the Son of Man coming back to life," he said. "Stay there until he has been raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus replied, "Assuredly, the Son of Man will be resurrected before the great day of God Almighty."
As for me, I tell you, Elias has come before, and the people did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will also subject the Son of Man to suffering.
And after the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist, Jesus heals a boy who has a demon.
As they came near to the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus. He knelt down before him.
"Oh Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He shakes a lot and suffers a lot. He always falls into fire or water."
I brought it for your students. As for what they couldn't see.
O people of disbelief and evil! Jesus said. "How long must I be with you? How long must I put up with you? Bring the child here."
Jesus ordered the devil to come out of the boy, and he came out. He was healed at the very moment.
And behold, the disciples were inside Jesus secretly. They asked, "Why could we not cast out the devil?"
Your faith is too small." What I am about to tell you is the truth. If you had faith as small as a mustard seed, it would be enough. You could say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there.' And it would move. Nothing would be impossible for you."
